window.docContents[4].resolve({"/part-1-foundations/ch04-transfer-principle/#ch04-transfer-principle":{"contents":"Computing directly with equivalence classes of sequences for every theorem of calculus would be exhausting. Chapter 4 builds the logical engine that makes ultrafilter indices disappear once and for all: Łoś's Theorem and the Transfer Principle.\n\nIn Ch04_TransferPrinciple.lean (2,754 lines), we construct deep embeddings of first-order terms (LTerm S) with partial function symbols (PartialFun S m, so division x / y and \\sqrt{x} carry explicit domains!) and bounded-quantifier formulas (LFormula S, supporting ∀ x ∈ A, φ and ∃ x ∈ A, φ). Every standard relation P \\subseteq S^k and partial function f lifts index-by-index to {}^*P and {}^*f, and structural induction proves:\n\n* Łoś's Theorem: {}^*\\varphi([r_1], \\dots, [r_m]) holds in {}^*S iff \\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} : \\varphi(r_1(n), \\dots, r_m(n))\\} \\in \\mathcal{F}.* Transfer Principle: Every first-order bounded sentence \\varphi is true over \\mathbb{R} if and only if its *-transform {}^*\\varphi is true over \\mathbb{R}^*!\n\nConsequently, identities like \\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1, the discreetness of hyperintegers (there is no N \\in \\mathbb{N}^* strictly between n and n + 1), and universal algebraic laws transfer automatically from \\mathbb{R} to \\mathbb{R}^*.\n\nsection\nopen Goldblatt.Ch04\n\n-- Deeply embedded first-order terms, bounded formulas, and complex transfer:\n#check LTerm\n#check PartialFun.IsTotal\n#check hypernat_discrete_between_nat\n#check exists_unlimited_mem_starSet_iff_unbounded\n#check complex_transfer_principle\nend\n\n\n","context":"Lectures on the Hyperreals — Interactive Lean 4 & Spatial Edition\u0009Part I — Foundations of the Hyperreal Line","header":"1.3. Chapter 4: Relational Structures & Łoś's Transfer Principle","id":"/part-1-foundations/ch04-transfer-principle/#ch04-transfer-principle"}});